WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.671
Six friends share a round pizza.

2
00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:10.521
Each one makes a single cut wherever they like, and takes the slice that ends at their own cut.

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.124
Random cuts sound fair, since nobody chose where anyone else would cut.

4
00:00:15.124 --> 00:00:18.596
Then the slices come out, and they're nothing like equal.

5
00:00:18.596 --> 00:00:20.057
Keep that pizza in mind.

6
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.821
Now the payments company.

7
00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:28.575
It spreads payments over its machines with a hash: a formula that turns a key into a big random-looking number.

8
00:00:28.575 --> 00:00:33.077
It divides that number by the number of machines, and keeps the remainder.

9
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.592
With four machines, the remainder picks machine 0, 1, 2 or 3.

10
00:00:37.592 --> 00:00:39.351
That spreads keys evenly.

11
00:00:39.351 --> 00:00:42.024
Until the day a fifth machine arrives.

12
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:47.900
Here are twenty hash values in a row, with their remainder for four machines and for five.

13
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.840
How many keep the same machine?

14
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:50.841
Pause and guess.

15
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:54.811
Only four.

16
00:00:54.811 --> 00:00:56.673
Zero, one, two and three.

17
00:00:56.673 --> 00:01:02.705
Every other key moves, so going from four machines to five moves 80% of the keys.

18
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:09.294
That's 80% of the data copied across the network, while the machines are still taking card payments.

19
00:01:09.294 --> 00:01:11.129
All that, to add one machine.

20
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.996
What we'd like is for the new machine to take its fair share, and for every other key to stay put.

21
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:18.471
Here's the idea.

22
00:01:18.471 --> 00:01:23.580
Lay every possible hash value around a circle, like the edge of the pizza.

23
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.237
That circle is the ring.

24
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.286
Each machine sits at the hash of its own name.

25
00:01:28.286 --> 00:01:31.679
That place is its token: the machine's cut in the pizza.

26
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:38.447
A key sits at the hash of the key, and belongs to the first machine you meet walking clockwise.

27
00:01:38.447 --> 00:01:39.544
Take key A-1001.

28
00:01:39.544 --> 00:01:42.492
Walk clockwise, and the first token is D's.

29
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:46.594
So each machine owns the arc that ends at its token.

30
00:01:46.594 --> 00:01:48.044
That arc is its slice.

31
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:50.402
Now a fifth machine joins, E.

32
00:01:50.402 --> 00:01:52.613
Its token lands inside D's arc.

33
00:01:52.613 --> 00:01:53.755
Which keys move?

34
00:01:53.755 --> 00:01:59.176
Only the ones on the arc just before E's token, and all of them come from D.

35
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.377
A-1001 is one of them, so it moves to E.

36
00:02:02.377 --> 00:02:04.981
A, B and C don't lose a single key.

37
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:06.847
That's consistent hashing.

38
00:02:06.847 --> 00:02:11.588
Consistent here means most keys stay where they were when the machines change.

39
00:02:11.588 --> 00:02:14.141
It has nothing to do with consistent data.

40
00:02:14.141 --> 00:02:18.883
Cassandra, a free database built to spread over many machines, is built on it.

41
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.227
So far so good.

42
00:02:20.227 --> 00:02:23.947
Now add a sixth machine, F, and look at the slices.

43
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:26.666
E owns 44% of the ring.

44
00:02:26.666 --> 00:02:27.936
C owns 1.6%.

45
00:02:27.936 --> 00:02:31.109
A fair share for six is 16.7%.

46
00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:31.850
Well...

47
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.552
E got the pizza, and C got a crumb.

48
00:02:35.867 --> 00:02:37.569
And it wasn't bad luck.

49
00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:42.750
A good hash is as good as random, and random points on a circle clump.

50
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.672
For six machines, the biggest gap averages about 41%.

51
00:02:46.672 --> 00:02:48.892
Thousands of keys average out.

52
00:02:48.892 --> 00:02:50.151
Six places don't.

53
00:02:50.333 --> 00:02:58.612
The machine with 44% stores 44% of the data and answers 44% of the requests, while its neighbour sits nearly idle.

54
00:02:58.612 --> 00:03:04.640
And buying a seventh machine doesn't fix it, because its token lands at random too.

55
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.390
The fix comes straight from the pizza.

56
00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:11.452
Instead of one cut each, every friend makes sixteen thin cuts.

57
00:03:11.452 --> 00:03:17.677
Each machine gets sixteen tokens, at the hashes of node A number 0, node A number 1, and so on.

58
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:27.001
Now a machine's share is the sum of many small arcs, and a sum of many random pieces lands much closer to its average than one piece does.

59
00:03:27.001 --> 00:03:29.903
These extra tokens are called virtual nodes.

60
00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:37.957
With sixteen each, the biggest share drops from 44% to 24.3%.

61
00:03:37.957 --> 00:03:43.193
With 64, it's 18.6%, and with 256, 17.4%.

62
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:45.413
But notice, it isn't smooth.

63
00:03:45.413 --> 00:03:49.515
Four tokens each gave 23.4%, a little better than sixteen.

64
00:03:49.515 --> 00:03:53.051
With only a few random tokens, luck still matters.

65
00:03:53.051 --> 00:03:57.577
That's why Cassandra doesn't place its sixteen tokens at random.

66
00:03:57.577 --> 00:04:01.749
It uses a token allocator, which this lesson comes back to.

67
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.048
And tokens fix one more thing.

68
00:04:04.048 --> 00:04:07.667
A machine twice as big can take twice as many tokens.

69
00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:08.994
So, the pizza.

70
00:04:08.994 --> 00:04:12.561
One random cut each gives slices nothing like equal.

71
00:04:12.561 --> 00:04:16.540
Many thin cuts each, and everyone gets about a fair slice.

72
00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:21.479
And whenever a machine joins, only the keys just before its tokens move.

73
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.090
One quick check.

74
00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.801
Back to one token each.

75
00:04:24.801 --> 00:04:29.562
A seventh machine, G, joins, and its token lands inside B's arc.

76
00:04:29.562 --> 00:04:31.644
Which machines give it keys?

77
00:04:31.644 --> 00:04:32.835
Pause and think.

78
00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:36.506
Only B.

79
00:04:36.506 --> 00:04:41.912
The new machine takes the arc just before its token, and that arc belonged to B.

80
00:04:41.912 --> 00:04:43.669
Every other key stays put.
